sentences.l:
He spoke, and the sons of Nestor he took as followers, the glorious one’s,
and Phyleus’-son Meges and Thoas and Meriones,
and Creon’s-son Lycomedes and Melanippus;

“I beg you, if any of my dear companions obeys me at least,
do not before bid me, of food or of drink,
satisfy my dear heart, since a fearsome grief comes upon me;

but the two sons of Atreus stayed, and heavenly Odysseus,
Nestor and Idomeneus and the old man, horse-driver Phoenix,
cheering him, thickly grieved;

But now you lie mangled, and my heart
is fasting from drink and food, though they are within,
for longing for you;

who now somewhere in Phthia sheds a tender tear
for lack of such a son; and I in a foreign land
for horrible Helen’s sake make war on the Trojans;
